# Whitelist-style: ignore everything, then re-include what belongs.
#
# The inverse of the usual additive list. Rather than naming what to exclude --
# which only ever covers the junk someone has already run into -- it names what
# to include, so anything unlisted stays out by default: editor and tool state,
# dependency and build directories, scratch output, stray secrets and logs.
#
# Two rules make it work, and both are easy to get wrong:
#
# 1. `*` has to be followed by `!*/`. A directory matched by `*` is excluded
#    outright, and no `!` pattern beneath an excluded directory can ever match.
#    `!*/` keeps directories traversable so the re-includes can reach inside
#    them. Without it every `!` line below silently does nothing, which is the
#    usual reason a whitelist .gitignore looks broken.
#
# 2. The last matching pattern wins. Re-includes come after the catch-all, and
#    anything that must stay out regardless of them goes last.
#
# The cost is that a file whose pattern is not listed is invisible to
# `git status`, with no warning. If something just created does not show up,
# this file is why:
#
#   git check-ignore -v <path>
#
# Re-including directories wholesale (`!/js/*`) rather than by extension keeps
# that cost low: new files in an already-listed directory need no edit here.
# Only a new top-level file, or a new directory, does.

# Ignore everything, but stay able to descend into directories.
*
!*/

# Top-level files.
!/.gitignore
!/.containerignore
!/Containerfile
!/biome.jsonc
!/index.html
!/*.md

# The site.
!/css/*
!/js/*

# Tests, CI, deploy.
!/test/*
!/.github/*.yml
!/.github/workflows/*.yml
!/deploy/*

# Kept out even inside the directories re-included above, since the last
# match wins. The catch-all already covers anything unlisted; these are the
# ones worth being explicit about.
.env*
*.pem
*.key
*.log
